### 2. what the two gates actually read, derived by reading them
A review round refuted the first version of this list. These are the lines:

gate C — scripts/dogfood/surface.sh:
  35:CSV="docs/audits/surface_audit.csv"
  50:BIN="$(bash "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/lib/binary.sh")"
  277:FIXTURE="tests/fixtures/dogfood/local-files.yaml"
gate D — scripts/dogfood/docs.sh:
  41:FIXTURES="tests/fixtures/dogfood"
  42:CSV="docs/audits/surface_audit.csv"
  51:COOKBOOK="${COOKBOOK:-${_common_abs}/../forjar-cookbook}"
  62:BIN="$(bash "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/lib/binary.sh")"
  82:README = "README.md"
  279:cargo_version = re.search(r'^version = "(.*)"', open("Cargo.toml", encoding="utf-8").read(),
and lib/binary.sh itself sources nothing further:
  source lines: 0

So the closure is: the built binary; scripts/dogfood/lib/binary.sh, which
both gates exec to resolve it; docs/audits/surface_audit.csv, which both
read; README.md, which only gate D runs; tests/fixtures/dogfood/, which
gate C compares two evaluations of and gate D runs everything against;
Cargo.toml, for gate D's version reconciliation; and a clone of
paiml/forjar-cookbook the workflow makes. The FIRST version of the
selection listed only the two gate scripts and the CSV, so the fixtures
and the shared resolver fell into the harmless arm — the fail-open
direction. Two of three lanes measured it.

