  All 14 of PMAT-542's cases are RED. One of PMAT-237's 13 is red too and that
  is honest rather than incidental: `every_heavy_job_runs_only_when_the_change_can_reach_it`
  reads the job names out of the workflow, and reverting the workflow puts back
  a job called `dogfood` where the case now expects `dogfood-surface` and
  `dogfood-guards`. The other 12 stay green, which is the other half of the
  claim: the CLASS is unchanged.

### 7. three numbers, and why each moved
FOURTEEN — the first scan, before any of this was implemented. It used a trigger
set narrower than what shipped: it did not count `.github/**` or
`scripts/dogfood/**` as reaching a gate.

TEN — the first implementation, measured. Conservative about `.github/**` and
about either gate's own script.

TWO — a review round then measured that the selection FAILED OPEN: both gates
run `scripts/dogfood/lib/binary.sh`, gate C compares two evaluations of
`tests/fixtures/dogfood/local-files.yaml`, and gate D runs everything against
the whole fixture tree — all under `scripts/` and `tests/`, which the harmless
arm names. The first fix selected the whole of `scripts/dogfood/` and
`tests/fixtures/`, and that collapsed the saving to two PRs in twenty-five,
because this repository develops harness.sh, tagged.sh, comply.sh and the rest
constantly and gate C and gate D read none of them.

SEVEN — the closure derived by reading the two scripts: `lib/*` (four files, so
a sibling either gate starts sourcing later is already covered), `surface.sh` for
C, `docs.sh` for D, and `tests/fixtures/*`, which is only the dogfood tree
today. Section 4 above is that run.

Every revision came from a measurement, and the fail-open one came from a
review round rather than from a test. That is what the round is for.

